From 410a717c196b4e5a23a34ec8168aef5dcdf3662e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: tomaszstaniak <nbw.designs@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 14:23:17 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 5/9] runtime: real OS layer for AROS, and the first run

The stubs become calls into C: write, exit, clock_gettime (both
clocks), nanosleep, sched_yield, AllocMem, and a per-M semaphore over a
pthread mutex and condition variable. Each crosses via asmcgocall to an
entry point in runtime/cgo/gcc_rt_aros.c, handed to package runtime
through a linknamed pointer exactly as _cgo_thread_start is. AROS has
no syscalls, so this makes runtime/cgo a hard dependency of the port;
an AROS executable is externally linked anyway.

The heap is one 256 MB arena from AllocMem taken in osinit, because
mem_sbrk.go needs sbrk results contiguous with the current break and
AllocMem cannot promise that call by call. initBloc is not used: it
starts the break at the end of the loaded module, meaningless for a
hunk-loaded ET_REL.

mOS.sema is a uintptr on purpose: semacreate is reached from lock2 and
notesleep, where a pointer write into m needs a write barrier those
callers forbid.

First run on the patched kernel reached runtime.abort from rt0_go's
TLS sanity check, because _cgo_init was nil: its initialiser lives in
callbacks_unix.go, which the unix build tag excludes on AROS. Tagged,
like the C files before it. AROS's crash requester symbolised the Go
function by name, which is what made this a ten-minute diagnosis.
